=== DNA Methylation Patterns

DNA methylation is the best-studied epigenetic layer in ME/CFS. Alterations are reported across several independent cohorts, but patterns are inconsistent across studies and remain early-stage as biomarkers:

    - Altered methylation at specific sites (promoter and gene-body CpG loci)
    - May reflect environmental exposures or disease state
    - Potential for stable biomarkers, but none yet clinically validated
    - Early-stage research

The most informative recent study shifted the question from "is methylation altered?" to "does it distinguish ME/CFS from other fatiguing illnesses?" An epigenome-wide association study of 188 women (73 healthy controls, 71 ME/CFS, 44 fibromyalgia) resolved two orthogonal methylation axes in peripheral blood mononuclear cells @Polli2026MethylationAxes. One axis separated ME/CFS from both fibromyalgia and controls and aligned with post-exertional malaise and cognitive symptoms; the other separated both patient groups from controls but did *not* separate ME/CFS from fibromyalgia, and aligned with broad symptom burden (widespread pain, sleep disturbance, visceral symptoms). Enrichment for both axes fell in neuroimmune, metabolic, cytokine, NF-κB, JAK-STAT, and TGF-β pathways.

(Severity applicability: unknown — the methylation cohort was not stratified by ME/CFS severity; the cohort was female-only, so generalization to male patients is untested. Evidence type: cross-sectional, correlational. Certainty: 0.44 — single peer-reviewed study, n=188, not yet replicated.)

#speculation(title: [Part of the ME/CFS Blood-Epigenetic Signal Is a General Illness-Burden Marker])[
  A substantial share of any ME/CFS blood-epigenetic signature may be a general marker of chronic illness burden rather than a disease-specific one. In the first cross-condition methylation comparison of ME/CFS with fibromyalgia, a single axis separated both ME/CFS and fibromyalgia from healthy controls but did not separate the two patient groups from each other, and it tracked broad symptom burden rather than core ME/CFS features @Polli2026MethylationAxes. The same concern applies to the 3D chromosome-conformation panel described in Section @sec:episwitch-3d-genomic, whose specificity was measured against healthy controls only.

  *Certainty:* 0.44 — one peer-reviewed cross-sectional study (n=188, female-only), single lab group, not yet replicated.

  *Limitations:* Cross-sectional design cannot separate cause from consequence; the burden axis and the disease-specific axis were derived in the same cohort, so their independence is not externally validated; the sample was female-only and not stratified by severity.

  *Falsifiability:* a cross-condition epigenome-wide study in which the ME/CFS-versus-fibromyalgia contrast is statistically indistinguishable from the ME/CFS-versus-control contrast (that is, no burden-only component) would refute the shared-burden interpretation.

  *Consequence:* if much of a blood-epigenetic signal reflects being chronically ill rather than the disease itself, then specificity — not sensitivity — is the binding constraint on every candidate ME/CFS blood test.
] <spec:methylation-shared-burden-axis>

A competing framework argues that DNA methylation — not 3D genome architecture — is the tractable unifying readout for ME/CFS and its overlapping conditions (long COVID, fibromyalgia, hypermobile EDS), on the grounds that methylation arrays are cheaper, more reproducible across labs, and already the substrate of the majority of ME/CFS epigenetic studies @Sharma2026MethylationUnifying. On present evidence the 3D-conformation layer has not demonstrated a marginal advantage over methylation: it is a single unreplicated vendor measurement, whereas methylation has already produced both a disease-specific and a symptom-burden axis in an independent cross-condition cohort. This is an argument about which measurement layer to prioritise for research, not evidence that either layer is a validated diagnostic.

Two implications follow. First, if a symptom-burden axis is a general feature of these overlapping conditions, it would give a molecular basis for the clinical blurring of ME/CFS, fibromyalgia, long COVID, and hypermobile EDS (Chapter @ch:comparative-nosology) — the diagnostic confusion would reflect a real shared chronic-illness state sitting on top of distinct disease-specific states, not merely a nosological failure. Second, and for diagnostic design, a useful test should score the disease-specific axis rather than the total signal; a classifier trained against healthy controls alone cannot distinguish the two components, and may therefore be measuring the burden axis that every chronic illness shares.


